If I would be forced to classify this game in a few words, I'd describe TinyKeep as a: "casual 'hack & slash'-game, with rogue-like elements". But then I'd be inaccurate. Rather, this is a lovingly created dungeon-romp in which you need to escape your confinements while surviving hordes of malicious enemies and enviroments, by using your wits, weapons *and* said enemies and enviroments. Still vague, right? Well picture it this way:
You awake in a dungeon, finding a note from a formerly-confined partner and an unlocked door. Your first frantic moments are spent dodging guards, releasing other prisoners (
), throwing over braziers (
) and finding gear to heal you and do battle with. Once this is accomplished, you realize escaping is going to take some time. And brawn. And cunning. And running away from stuff.
The dungeon's you'll traverse are quite small and their rooms are seldom empty. The maps are randomly-generated, so you'll never play the same floor twice. That being said, the levels themselves *do* follow a pre-designed path, allowing the game's story to unfold. For instance:
. Enemies re-spawn at random locations as well, so you always need to be on your toes. The small dungeons and respawning enemies help keep the game's flow up in a positive way. My only gripe with the game is the lack of a save-feature. Every time you start a new game, you wake up in your original cell. I think permadeath has always been part of the game's original design, but hopefully saving will be implemented in the future. In the meantime, I'll certainly be putting more time in TinyKeep.

